> **Dinesh:** I was wondering if this same technique could be useful to exaggerate the chemical hardness differential by optical means. Jeff made the point that it would be difficult to differentiate between nodes and anti-nodes if you simply zapped the entire emulsion. It occured to me that if you could use the already-differentially-hardened internal matrix to cause differential absorption of some material - say "dye" for lack of a better word, then zap the entire emulsion with a form of light that has no actinic response on it's own, but will produce actinic reactions in the "dye". Since the amount of "dye" in the matrix will vary locally according to the (chemically induced) hardness at that location, the amount of actinic radiation re-radiated by the "dye" will also vary in strength. Since the resulting optical hardness will depend on the strength of the actinic radiation, so will the resulting hardness.


The dye thing based on methylene blue, I had in mind, would look like this:

reversal bleach an exposed-developed SH emulsion (say PFG-03;

insert the plate into a MB solution + electron donor (the key idea being that the MB dye won't stick on SH grains). Have it dry and tuned to reconstruct under red wavelength;

adapt the plate to reconstruct the image under HeNe illumination;

long HeNe exposure;

develop the plate in a weak dichromate solution. That should result in crosslinking of the non-silver area.

process a la DCG.
